25,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It seems the catalogue-style projects have a lasting legacy, whereas the mechanism-focused ones often leave questions unanswered.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
26,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"We have a shortage of phenotyping technicians, but plenty of interest in modelling and code. However, senior analysts are currently abroad.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
27,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"The animal facilities are in good shape, but the spectroscopy suite had a significant lapse in service last year.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
28,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Computing is currently free, but animal-unit costs have spiked. Also, shipping costs are rising while per-diems remain frozen.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
29,2,1,talk,Midori,,"Field sites have been hit hard by disasters, though indoor facilities are stable. Fortunately, the repository turnaround is now very fast.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
30,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Ryuji, does the lack of phenotyping staff make you wary of projects relying on heavy manual data collection?","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"Yes, it does. Projects requiring specialized technical hands for biological sampling are at higher risk than those based on simulation.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Benjamin, the rise in animal-unit costs might make some proposals financially precarious if they aren't centrally subsidized.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,Precisely. Any project heavily dependent on the vivarium will see its purchasing power shrink rapidly.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Victoria, if the spectroscopy suite is unstable, we should be cautious about projects that rely solely on that instrumentation.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, the field site instability is concerning. Perhaps we should favor projects that can be conducted in controlled indoor environments.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,I agree with Shizue. The combination of staffing gaps and field risks makes outdoor biological work quite risky.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,3,talk,Victoria,,I am wondering if the computing agreement's lack of charge makes simulation-heavy projects the safest bet for the budget.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It certainly makes them more attractive, provided the analysts are actually available to do the work.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,3,talk,Midori,,That is a fair point. We must balance the lack of cost with the actual availability of the experts.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
